{"acceptedResponses":["E"],"answer":"E","bank":"amc","batch":"NA","choiceOrder":["A","B","C","D","E"],"choices":{"A":"\\(\\dfrac{2ab}{a+b}\\gt\\sqrt{ab}\\gt\\dfrac{a+b}{2}\\)","B":"\\(\\sqrt{ab}\\gt\\dfrac{2ab}{a+b}\\gt\\dfrac{a+b}{2}\\)","C":"\\(\\dfrac{2ab}{a+b}\\gt\\dfrac{a+b}{2}\\gt\\sqrt{ab}\\)","D":"\\(\\dfrac{a+b}{2}\\gt\\dfrac{2ab}{a+b}\\gt\\sqrt{ab}\\)","E":"\\(\\dfrac{a+b}{2}\\gt\\sqrt{ab}\\gt\\dfrac{2ab}{a+b}\\)"},"choicesType":"text","domain":[],"editorialNote":"","figureSources":{},"id":"1952_AMC_12_Q45","knowledgeGraph":{"assessedIds":["cnm_h021"],"contentHash":"4cbeea7465713476b5146082f9cfb83c5fec6da8fc139ac847baf6d23f26ac4e","flags":[],"reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"am-gm-and-positive-division","label":"Apply strict AM-GM","methodDirectNodeIds":{"am-gm-and-positive-division":["cnm_h021","cnm_096"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h021","cnm_096"]},{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"positive-square-difference","label":"Use a positive square","methodDirectNodeIds":{"positive-square-difference":["cnm_148","cnm_h019","cnm_096"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_148","cnm_h019","cnm_096"]},{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_168","cnm_169"],"id":"positive-square-comparison","label":"Use a positive square","methodDirectNodeIds":{"positive-square-comparison":["cnm_148","cnm_096"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_148","cnm_096","cnm_168","cnm_169"]}],"status":"mapped","supportIds":["cnm_096","cnm_168","cnm_169"],"version":"1.0.0"},"level":"AMC 12","number":45,"question":"If \\(a\\) and \\(b\\) are two unequal positive numbers, then:","questionType":"text","rejectedAnswers":[],"responseType":"choice","schemaVersion":"3.0","skill":[],"solution":"The same inequality will give both comparisons. Since \\(a\\) and \\(b\\) are positive and unequal, their square roots are unequal. Therefore\n\\[\n(\\sqrt a-\\sqrt b)^2\n=a+b-2\\sqrt{ab}>0.\n\\]\nThus \\(a+b>2\\sqrt{ab}\\). Dividing by \\(2\\) gives\n\\[\n\\frac{a+b}{2}>\\sqrt{ab}.\n\\]\nTo obtain the other comparison, multiply \\(a+b>2\\sqrt{ab}\\) by the positive quantity \\(\\sqrt{ab}/(a+b)\\). The inequality direction stays the same, giving\n\\[\n\\sqrt{ab}>\\frac{2ab}{a+b}.\n\\]\nHere \\(a+b>0\\), so the denominator cannot be zero. Combining the two strict comparisons gives\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{a+b}{2}>\\sqrt{ab}>\\frac{2ab}{a+b}},\n\\]\nwhich is choice E.","solutionAvailability":"present","solutionMethods":[{"directNodeIds":["cnm_148","cnm_096"],"explanation":"The same inequality will give both comparisons. Since \\(a\\) and \\(b\\) are positive and unequal, their square roots are unequal. Therefore\n\\[\n(\\sqrt a-\\sqrt b)^2\n=a+b-2\\sqrt{ab}>0.\n\\]\nThus \\(a+b>2\\sqrt{ab}\\). Dividing by \\(2\\) gives\n\\[\n\\frac{a+b}{2}>\\sqrt{ab}.\n\\]\nTo obtain the other comparison, multiply \\(a+b>2\\sqrt{ab}\\) by the positive quantity \\(\\sqrt{ab}/(a+b)\\). The inequality direction stays the same, giving\n\\[\n\\sqrt{ab}>\\frac{2ab}{a+b}.\n\\]\nHere \\(a+b>0\\), so the denominator cannot be zero. Combining the two strict comparisons gives\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{a+b}{2}>\\sqrt{ab}>\\frac{2ab}{a+b}},\n\\]\nwhich is choice E.","id":"positive-square-comparison","label":"Use a positive square","legacyRouteId":"positive-square-comparison","provenance":{"evidence":[{"label":"Accepted primary worked-solution review","path":"../database/knowledge-graph/evidence/solutions-2026-11-06/1952_AMC_12_Q45.json","sha256":"43472f0e1cbfcf3c23c90bf5a0300ce48c5385eb88b3ac004588f7909ea598d3"}],"license":"CC-BY-NC-SA-4.0","licenseURL":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/","reviewStatus":"AI-authored and independently AI-reviewed; not teacher reviewed","source":"Original Math Practice solution","sourceRef":"amc/1952_AMC_12_Q45#positive-square-comparison","sourceURL":"https://github.com/rqzhu-aide/math-practice"}},{"directNodeIds":["cnm_h021","cnm_096"],"id":"am-gm-and-positive-division","label":"Apply strict AM-GM","legacyRouteId":"am-gm-and-positive-division","provenance":{"evidence":[{"label":"Legacy graph an